# --- Notes for this consumer (djl-executorch-engine) ---
# Linux ships `devtools`: it carries the ExecuTorch event tracer, which backs the engine's opt-in
# profiling, and a devtools runtime with no tracer attached costs +138 KB of shipped .so and under
# 0.35% steady state. `logging` and `bare` rows stay live for native/build_variants.sh benchmarking;
# `bare` is the only logging-free comparison point, since devtools is built WITH logging.
# windows ships `logging` for now. Devtools rows exist for Windows as of v1.4.1-3, so provisioning
# it is a build.sh list edit plus a test, not a redesign -- the engine gates on the
# devtoolsAvailable() capability query, never on a platform name.
# Every platform publishes TWO Windows rows per variant: `windows-x86_64` is /MD (dynamic CRT, for
# CPython extensions) and `windows-x86_64-static` is /MT. This engine resolves the -static row so
# the shipped DLL needs no VC++ redistributable; the /MD row is kept because
# native/tests/cmake_resolution.sh asserts the row is a real choice, not a hardcode.
# Devtools tarballs alone install include/executorch/devtools/** and include/flatcc/**, and their
# BUILDINFO carries event_tracer=on (logging and bare carry off). native/CMakeLists.txt reads BOTH
# that key and `TARGET etdump`, failing configure if they disagree: upstream's etdump install rule
# is unguarded, so target presence alone is not a promise the tracer was compiled in.
# The ET_RUNTIME_OPENVINO_* rows are consumed by native/build.sh, which stages a bundle into the
# opt-in OpenVINO jar. It reads the PER-PLATFORM rows and keys them on the platform identity, never
# on the pin row: the windows-x86_64-static row is an ALIAS whose value is a CMake variable
# reference, which shell cannot dereference. The singular ET_RUNTIME_OPENVINO_{PLATFORM,URL,SHA256}
# vars below are deprecated upstream and are deliberately not read here.
# Bump procedure: replace this whole file with the EtRuntimePin.cmake asset from the next
# `v<etver>-<pkgrev>` Repo A release, then re-apply this comment block. The SHA256 change is the
# supply-chain review gate.
